 Monday ✅.

 Today's newsletter is 862 words, a 3½-minute read. 

 📈 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed up 0.7%.

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: The PHLX Semiconductor Sector Index (+2.9%), as chip stocks and other AI-related shares rose after Anthropic's IPO filing.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Claude is (officially) going public 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic publicly filed for its highly anticipated IPO this morning, setting the stage for it to become the first pure-play AI model developer to hit the stock market, potentially before Thanksgiving.

 By the numbers: The Claude maker hasn't said yet how much it plans to raise in the offering, but there's speculation it could seek a record $100 billion at a $2 trillion valuation, Axios' Dan Primack writes .

 💸 It reported an $80.75 billion net loss on $16.29 billion in revenue for the first half of 2026. It didn't report Q3 numbers, but given that they've been leaked , don't be surprised if they emerge in an amended filing before pricing. 🐜 Its shares would trade on Nasdaq under the ticker "ANT." ⚠️ Zoom out: Recent reports detailed some hair-raising risk warnings, and today's official filing did not disappoint.

 The over 90 pages of risk factors contain some of the wildest in IPO history, Axios' Madison Mills writes — a jarring collision of Wall Street legalese and what some see as Silicon Valley sci-fi, including catastrophic threats to humanity, blackmailing AI models and $750 billion in computing commitments. They also include more typical financial cautions, including massive computing commitments, the risk of overestimating or underestimating future demand, and potential defaults if the company can't meet its financial obligations. The intrigue: Anthropic also warned investors that it may continue to be a target of the Trump administration — and that it has no plans to change its behavior, Axios' Maria Curi writes .

 Later this afternoon, the FTC sent demands for Anthropic executives to testify in its consumer harm probe, an agency spokesperson tells Maria . The big picture: This IPO is likely to become a referendum on the business of AI, even if it's just a securities offering from a single company.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. The great AI cash grab 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Allie Carl/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic is not the only company looking to raise piles of cash for its AI buildout.

 Before Anthropic's filing hit, Meta and SpaceX were already making headlines with announcements of their own, Axios' Pete Gannon writes . 💰 Zoom in: Meta said it's planning to raise up to $95 billion through equity offerings, broken into an initial $35 billion sale of stock, and another $60 billion in non-voting shares.

 SpaceX, meanwhile, announced a $40 billion debt financing for chips. 💪 The intrigue: SpaceX said that the deal "represents the largest committed GPU financing" — a bit of a flex after Elon Musk yesterday tweeted that debt markets have "almost entirely shut off access to money for any company that doesn't have substantial revenue independent of AI data centers."

 The big picture: As Dan Primack noted, AI giants need additional investment, as massive revenue isn't keeping pace with spending.

 
 
